# Cursor Rules Format

Rules for writing and formatting Cursor rules.

<rule>
name: cursor_rules_format
description: Standards for writing and formatting Cursor rules
filters:
  # Match any .mdc files in the .cursor/rules directory
  - type: file_extension
    pattern: "\\.mdc$"
  # Match files that look like Cursor rules
  - type: content
    pattern: "(?s)<rule>.*?</rule>"
  # Match file creation events
  - type: event
    pattern: "file_create"

actions:
  - type: suggest
    message: |
      When creating Cursor rules:

      1. File Header:
         ```
         ---
         description: Rule Description
         globs: pattern1, pattern2
         ---
         # Rule Title
         ```

      2. Rule Structure:
         ```yaml
         <rule>
         name: rule_name
         description: Detailed description of the rule
         filters:
           - type: filter_type
             pattern: "regex_pattern"
         actions:
           - type: action_type
             message: |
               Detailed message with:
               - Bullet points
               - Code blocks
               - Examples
         examples:
           - input: |
               Example input
             output: "Expected output"
         metadata:
           priority: high|medium|low
           version: x.y.z
         </rule>
         ```

      3. Required Components:
         - YAML frontmatter with description and globs
         - Markdown title
         - Rule tag with name and description
         - At least one filter
         - At least one action
         - Examples showing good/bad cases
         - Metadata with priority and version

      4. Best Practices:
         - Use clear, descriptive names
         - Include comprehensive examples
         - Use code blocks for formatting
         - Keep descriptions concise but informative
         - Use bullet points for readability
         - Include both positive and negative examples

      5. File Naming:
         - Use kebab-case for filenames
         - Make names descriptive of the rule's purpose
         - Always use .mdc extension

examples:
  - input: |
      # Bad: Missing components
      <rule>
      name: bad_rule
      </rule>

      # Good: Complete rule
      ---
      description: Example Rule
      globs: *.py
      ---
      # Example Rule

      <rule>
      name: good_rule
      description: A well-formatted rule
      filters:
        - type: file_extension
          pattern: "\\.py$"
      actions:
        - type: suggest
          message: |
            This is a good example
      examples:
        - input: "bad code"
          output: "good code"
      metadata:
        priority: high
        version: 1.0
      </rule>
    output: "Correctly formatted rule"

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.0
</rule> 